@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Booking, Cancellation & Refund Policy | Karnel Travel Guide";
    ViewData["Description"] = "Draft information about travel inquiries, booking requests, demo payments, cancellations and refunds at Karnel Travel Guide.";
}

<main class="policy-page">
    <header class="policy-hero">
        <div class="container policy-hero__inner">
            <nav class="policy-breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
                <a href="@Url.Action("Index", "Home")">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Booking &amp; Cancellation</span>
            </nav>
            <p class="policy-eyebrow">Policies &amp; information</p>
            <h1>Booking, Cancellation &amp; Refund</h1>
            <p class="policy-intro">This draft describes the current booking-request and demo-payment workflow. It does not create refund promises or deadlines that the business has not approved.</p>
            <p class="policy-status">Draft for business review. Cancellation windows, refund rules, fees and processing times are still to be confirmed.</p>
        </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container policy-layout">
        <aside class="policy-toc" aria-label="On this page">
            <p>On this page</p>
            <ol>
                <li><a href="#terms">Key terms</a></li>
                <li><a href="#request">Request process</a></li>
                <li><a href="#confirmation">Confirmation</a></li>
                <li><a href="#payment">Payment conditions</a></li>
                <li><a href="#cancellation">Cancellation and refund</a></li>
                <li><a href="#changes">Changes and disruption</a></li>
                <li><a href="#responsibilities">Responsibilities</a></li>
            </ol>
        </aside>

        <article class="policy-content">
            <section id="terms">
                <h2>1. Inquiry, booking request and confirmed booking</h2>
                <p>An <strong>inquiry</strong> asks for information or assistance. A <strong>booking request</strong> submits proposed service, date, party and contact details for review. A <strong>confirmed booking</strong> is a request that has been accepted and confirmed through the applicable Karnel Travel Guide workflow.</p>
                <p>Submitting a form does not guarantee availability, a price, a place, a departure, accommodation or any other service until confirmation is communicated.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="request">
                <h2>2. How booking requests work</h2>
                <p>The current application validates the selected published service, requested date, configured booking window, contact requirements and party limits. Tour requests may also depend on a selected departure and available capacity. Requests can be returned for clarification or declined when the service cannot be arranged.</p>
                <p>Customers should review names, contact details, dates, party size and service selection before submitting. A customer may contact the business through the inquiry page if a request needs correction.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="confirmation">
                <h2>3. Booking confirmation</h2>
                <p>Booking statuses are managed through the application’s review workflow. A successful demo payment currently moves an eligible booking to a pending administrator-approval state; administrator review is still required before a service is treated as approved.</p>
                <p>Where a booking is approved and confirmed, the application may generate a customer ticket and send a confirmation message if the configured email service is available. The business should define the final confirmation channel and wording.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="payment">
                <h2>4. Payment conditions</h2>
                <p>The current project contains a clearly identified demo payment flow. The amount is calculated from the booking on the server, and demo transactions record simulated outcomes such as paid, failed or cancelled. This is not a statement that a live payment gateway, card settlement or third-party payment arrangement is available.</p>
                <p>Customers should not treat a demo transaction as a guarantee of service delivery. The business must approve the live payment provider, currencies, receipts, fees, payment timing and customer-facing payment terms before launch.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="cancellation">
                <h2>5. Cancellation and refund</h2>
                <p>Customers should submit a cancellation request through the available account or contact workflow and include the relevant booking reference. The application can record a customer-cancelled demo payment and a cancelled booking in the applicable workflow.</p>
                <p>The project does not establish approved cancellation deadlines, refund percentages, non-refundable components, administrative charges, refund processing times or payment-provider rules. No such promise is made by this draft.</p>
                <div class="policy-callout">
                    <strong>Business confirmation required:</strong> define cancellation windows, who may cancel, provider-specific rules, refund eligibility, partial refunds, fees, currency treatment, processing times and the contact process before publishing this section as an official policy.
                </div>
                <p>When an administrator rejects a booking with a paid demo transaction, the current application records a demo refund-pending status. That implementation state is not a completed real-world refund commitment.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="changes">
                <h2>6. Booking modifications and service changes</h2>
                <p>Changes to dates, travellers, services or other request details may require a new review and may affect availability or price. Customers should not assume that a requested change has been accepted until written confirmation is provided.</p>
                <p>Weather, road conditions, provider availability, operational constraints and other circumstances may cause delays, substitutions or changes. The business should document its customer notification, alternative-service and refund approach for each provider category.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="responsibilities">
                <h2>7. Third-party providers and customer responsibilities</h2>
                <p>Accommodation, transport, restaurant, tour and other services may be supplied by independent providers. Their terms, safety requirements, availability and cancellation conditions may apply in addition to this website’s process.</p>
                <p>Customers are responsible for checking passports, visas, permits, health guidance, insurance, luggage, accessibility needs, emergency contacts and current travel conditions. For assistance, use the <a href="@Url.Action("Index", "Contact")">contact and inquiry page</a> before relying on a booking request.</p>
            </section>
        </article>
    </div>
</main>
